<h1>state machine</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="modelOfComputation.html"><em>model of computation</em></a> consisting of a (possibly infinite) set of <a href="state.html"><em>states</em></a>, a set of <a href="startstate.html"><em>start states</em></a>, an input <a href="alphabet.html"><em>alphabet</em></a>, and a <a href="transitionfn.html"><em>transition function</em></a> which maps input symbols and current states to a <a href="nextstate.html"><em>next state</em></a>.  Usually understood to be a <a href="finiteStateMachine.html"><em>finite state machine</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="modelOfComputation.html"><em>model of computation</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="finiteStateMachine.html"><em>finite state machine</em></a>.
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

